     "transcript": [
      {
       "start": 3.4,
       "end": 10.644,
       "text": "Order matters. The wrong way: pick the highest-affinity pair, then check the capacity.",
       "sceneId": "a01-filter"
      },
      {
       "start": 10.644,
       "end": 19.222,
       "text": "At step two of G01 at capacity 2, the largest, AB–C, is over capacity, so it stops at 4 clusters, cut 13.",
       "sceneId": "a01-filter"
      },
      {
       "start": 19.222,
       "end": 26.244,
       "text": "The right way filters first: D–E can still merge, and it stops at 3 clusters, cut 8.",
       "sceneId": "a01-filter"
      },
      {
       "start": 26.244,
       "end": 29.711,
       "text": "Same graph; only the order differs.",
       "sceneId": "a01-filter"
      },
      {
       "start": 30.111,
       "end": 38.022,
       "text": "Three twin rooms: every node has size 2, capacity 3, K = 2. The bound is 2; nothing is ruled out.",
       "sceneId": "a02-bound"
      },
      {
       "start": 38.022,
       "end": 43.711,
       "text": "Yet any two together make 4, over 3: at least 3 clusters.",
       "sceneId": "a02-bound"
      },
      {
       "start": 43.711,
       "end": 50.511,
       "text": "Meeting the bound is not feasibility. Deciding whether it fits is bin packing itself.",
       "sceneId": "a02-bound"
      },
      {
       "start": 50.911,
       "end": 55.378,
       "text": "How costly is exhaustive search? Ways to group n nodes:",
       "sceneId": "a03-bell"
      },
      {
       "start": 55.378,
       "end": 60.844,
       "text": "5 nodes, 52; 10 nodes, already over a hundred thousand.",
       "sceneId": "a03-bell"
      },
      {
       "start": 60.844,
       "end": 65.644,
       "text": "15 and 20 nodes quickly reach tens of trillions.",
       "sceneId": "a03-bell"
      },
      {
       "start": 65.644,
       "end": 71.444,
       "text": "So this course uses exhaustive search only as a check at 10 nodes or fewer.",
       "sceneId": "a03-bell"
      },
      {
       "start": 71.844,
       "end": 78.2,
       "text": "On greedy_trap, greedy stops at 3 clusters, cut 8.",
       "sceneId": "a04-samek"
      },
      {
       "start": 78.2,
       "end": 83.222,
       "text": "The feasible two-cluster plan AC | BD has cut 10.",
       "sceneId": "a04-samek"
      },
      {
       "start": 83.222,
       "end": 88.467,
       "text": "Greedy's cut is smaller, because it has one more cluster.",
       "sceneId": "a04-samek"
      },
      {
       "start": 88.467,
       "end": 92.6,
       "text": "Compare cuts only at the same number of clusters.",
       "sceneId": "a04-samek"
      },
      {
       "start": 93.0,
       "end": 99.8,
       "text": "How far off can a legal greedy result be? Scale the suboptimal graph.",
       "sceneId": "a05-gap"
      },
      {
       "start": 99.8,
       "end": 103.044,
       "text": "M = 10: 18 versus 11.",
       "sceneId": "a05-gap"
      },
      {
       "start": 103.044,
       "end": 106.956,
       "text": "M = 100: 198 versus 101.",
       "sceneId": "a05-gap"
      },
      {
       "start": 106.956,
       "end": 111.533,
       "text": "M = 1000: 1998 versus 1001.",
       "sceneId": "a05-gap"
      },
      {
       "start": 111.533,
       "end": 117.667,
       "text": "Greedy is 2(M − 1), optimal M + 1: the gap approaches twice.",
       "sceneId": "a05-gap"
      },
      {
       "start": 118.067,
       "end": 124.422,
       "text": "With every size 1, capacity 3 gives ABC | DE, cut 3.",
       "sceneId": "a06-area"
      },
      {
       "start": 124.422,
       "end": 130.333,
       "text": "Change only A to 2, and the result becomes AB | CDE, cut 5.",
       "sceneId": "a06-area"
      },
      {
       "start": 130.333,
       "end": 136.022,
       "text": "Real cells differ in area; capacity must count the sum of areas.",
